    contentEn: `<p>Besalú is around 1 hour 30 minutes from Barcelona by private car, a distance of roughly 130 km, and the trip works as a full round trip rather than a one-way transfer — your driver brings you out for the day and takes you back to Barcelona afterward. Given how small the village itself is, many visitors use the flexibility of a private vehicle to combine Besalú with another stop in the same day, most commonly Figueres or a spot further along the Costa Brava.</p>
<p>Because there's no direct train service into the village, a private car is the most straightforward way to get there without relying on connecting buses. You can book the day through <a href="/get-a-quote/">get a quote</a>, and it's worth mentioning if you'd like to pair Besalú with a second town, since your driver can plan the route and timing accordingly.</p>
<h2>About Besalú</h2>
<p>Besalú is a small medieval village in Girona province, and what sets it apart is how intact it's remained — this isn't a restored old quarter surrounded by a modern town, but a genuinely well-preserved medieval settlement that's stayed largely as it was, having served as the capital of an independent county in the early Middle Ages before later being absorbed into the wider County of Barcelona. Its best-known landmark is a fortified Romanesque bridge that crosses the Fluvià river in a distinctive angled shape, with a defensive gate partway along its length — a design that reflects its original purpose as a controlled crossing point rather than a purely decorative structure. The bridge leads directly into the old town's stone streets and arcaded square, and it's the image most associated with Besalú, appearing on postcards and in countless photographs of the village.</p>
<p>The village also has one of the more unusual historical sites in the region: a well-preserved medieval Jewish quarter, or call, including a mikveh, a ritual bath used for Jewish purification rites, discovered beneath a private house and now one of relatively few such structures preserved in Spain. The mikveh dates back to the medieval Jewish community that once lived in Besalú, before the expulsion of Jews from Spain at the end of the fifteenth century brought that community to an end, and the site gives a rare, tangible sense of a part of Spanish history that's otherwise been largely erased from most towns. Between the bridge, the old town, and the Jewish quarter, Besalú can be seen thoroughly in a couple of hours, which is part of why it's so often paired with somewhere else on the same trip.</p>
<p>Beyond the bridge and the mikveh, the old town holds a scatter of other Romanesque and Gothic buildings worth a slow look rather than a rushed pass-through — the church of Sant Vicenç, with its carved capitals and rose window, the remains of a former medieval hospital, and Casa Cornellà, a well-preserved Romanesque house on the main square that's among the oldest civil buildings still standing in Catalonia. None of these require much time individually, but together they explain why Besalú is often described as feeling more like a small open-air museum of medieval building styles than a single monument with a village attached.</p>
<h3>A Village Built for Walking</h3>
<p>Besalú's old town is compact enough to see almost entirely on foot within a short visit, with stone streets, arcaded porticos, and small squares laid out on a scale that predates cars altogether. The Church of Sant Pere, a Romanesque monastery church near the bridge, and the remains of a former synagogue add to the sense of a village where several centuries of history sit within a few minutes' walk of each other. Because there's little modern development within the old core, walking through Besalú feels less like visiting a historic site and more like moving through a place that simply never expanded much beyond its medieval footprint.</p>
<p>This compactness is exactly why the village works so well as a shorter stop rather than a full day's destination — most visitors can take in the main sights unhurried and still have the rest of the day free, which is part of what makes it such a natural match for a private, flexible itinerary rather than a standalone excursion. There's also no real need for a map once you're inside the old town, since almost every street eventually leads back toward the bridge or the main square, which makes Besalú an easy place to wander without much planning.</p>
<h3>Pairing Besalú With Another Stop</h3>
<p>Because Besalú is compact, a private vehicle lets you treat it as one part of a larger day rather than the sole destination — many visitors combine it with Figueres or another town in the same area, using the drive between them productively instead of losing time on separate return trips to Barcelona. Your driver can plan the order and timing based on which stop you'd rather spend more time at, and can adjust on the day if one place turns out to hold your interest longer than expected.</p>
<p>This kind of flexible, multi-stop day is exactly what our <a href="/hourly-chauffeur/">hourly chauffeur</a> service is designed for, and it suits families or small groups who want to see more than one place without the logistics of separate bookings, since everyone stays together in one vehicle for the whole day rather than juggling separate tickets and timetables. For general planning around the wider Barcelona area, see our <a href="/barcelona/">Barcelona</a> guide.</p>`,
